{
  "topic": "Linear Transformations and Matrices",
  "target_audience": "High school students (grades 11-12) with basic algebra knowledge",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "section_1",
      "title": "Introduction: What is a Linear Transformation?",
      "content": "Define a linear transformation as a function that preserves vector addition and scalar multiplication. Use a simple 2D example with a grid and a vector. Show visually how a linear transformation maps the grid lines to parallel, evenly spaced lines. Emphasize key properties: T(u+v)=T(u)+T(v), T(cv)=cT(v).",
      "example": "Consider a 2D vector (1,0) and (0,1). Apply transformation T(x,y) = (2x, y) - stretch horizontally. Show original grid and transformed grid."
    },
    {
      "id": "section_2",
      "title": "From Transformation to Matrix: The Standard Matrix",
      "content": "Explain that any linear transformation from R^n to R^m can be represented by an m×n matrix. The columns of the matrix are the images of the standard basis vectors. Show how to construct the matrix for a given transformation.",
      "example": "For T(x,y) = (2x+y, x-3y), find the matrix by applying T to e1=(1,0) and e2=(0,1): T(e1)=(2,1), T(e2)=(1,-3). So matrix A = [[2,1],[1,-3]]."
    },
    {
      "id": "section_3",
      "title": "Matrix-Vector Multiplication: Applying the Transformation",
      "content": "Show how multiplying a matrix by a vector performs the transformation. Use animated visual: vector as arrow, matrix multiplication as linear combination of columns. Connect to the definition: Av = v1*col1 + v2*col2.",
      "example": "Using A = [[2,1],[1,-3]] and v=(3,2), compute Av = 3*(2,1) + 2*(1,-3) = (6,3)+(2,-6)=(8,-3). Show original vector (3,2) and transformed vector (8,-3) on grid."
    },
    {
      "id": "section_4",
      "title": "Geometric Interpretation: Seeing Linear Transformations",
      "content": "Visualize common 2D transformations: scaling, rotation, shear, reflection. Show how each corresponds to a specific matrix. Use animations to morph a shape (e.g., a cat silhouette) under these transformations.",
      "example": "Rotation by 90°: matrix [[0,-1],[1,0]]. Show a cat image rotated. Shear: matrix [[1,1],[0,1]] - show cat sheared horizontally."
    },
    {
      "id": "section_5",
      "title": "Composition and Matrix Multiplication",
      "content": "Explain that applying one transformation after another corresponds to multiplying their matrices. Emphasize order matters. Show composition of two transformations (e.g., rotate then scale) and the resulting matrix.",
      "example": "Let T1 be rotation 90°: A1=[[0,-1],[1,0]], T2 be scaling by 2: A2=[[2,0],[0,2]]. Composition T2∘T1 has matrix A2*A1 = [[0,-2],[2,0]]. Apply to vector (1,0): first T1 gives (0,1), then T2 gives (0,2). Matrix multiplication gives same."
    },
    {
      "id": "section_6",
      "title": "Summary and Key Takeaways",
      "content": "Recap: linear transformations preserve vector operations, can be represented by matrices, matrix multiplication composes transformations. Emphasize the visual connection between algebra and geometry.",
      "example": "Show a quick animation of a shape undergoing a sequence of transformations, with matrices displayed alongside, reinforcing the link."
    }
  ]
}
